  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nCurrent approaches rely on SLSQP and local search optimized for fixed resolutions. This strategy uses a versatile optimization framework capable of adapting to different grid sizes and employing multiple search techniques simultaneously. Unlike the previous method, which primarily doubles the grid size, we'll implement:\n- Progressive grid expansion with careful transition\n- Global optimization using simulated annealing to escape local minima\n- Local refinement with gradient-based SLSQP for precision\n- Robust constraint handling via projection\n- Parameterized search for smoothness and regularization\nThe key advantage is escaping local optima more effectively by dynamically adjusting these elements.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nMinimize:\n    C\u2085 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nSubject to:\n    h(x) \u2208 [0,1] for all x\n    \u222bh(x)dx = 1 over [0,2]\n\nDiscrete formulation:\n    Let h \u2208 \u211d\u207f with \u2211h = n/2\n    \u2211\u2080\u207f\u207b\u00b9 h[i] = n/2\n    C\u2085 = (max correlation between h and 1-h) * (2/n)\n\nWe introduce a unified framework that can be parameterized directly through search algorithm settings.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\nThe execution follows this flow:\n\n- Start from initial configuration (if provided) or random projection\n- Validate and project initial candidate to satisfy 0\u2264h\u22641 and sum=0.5*n\n- Adaptive grid expansion (n, 2n, 4n...) with:\n    * Gradient-based expansion (clip and project) \n    * Smoothness adjustment for better optimization\n- Multi-stage optimization:\n    1. Initialization: if no initial provided, generate random candidate\n    2. Global optimization: simulated annealing with bounds\n    3. Local refinement: SLSQP with projection\n    4. Final polishing: gradient descent with exact projection\n- Maintain best candidate throughout all stages\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\nThe algorithm uses:\n- Temperature schedule: t = t\u2080 * (1 - (iterations/max_iter)) for annealing\n- Mutation rate: dynamically adjusted based on objective improvement\n- Cooling factor: adaptive based on plateau detection\n- SLSQP iterations: heuristic based on grid size\n- Constraint handling: exact projection after each iteration\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\nDominant costs are grid size and optimization iterations. We'll:\n- Monitor wall time continuously and adjust iterations accordingly\n- Use checkpointing for long computations\n- Set failure recovery to projection of best candidate\n- Automatically tune algorithm parameters based on grid size\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n- Validate: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, sum(h)=n/2\n- Check objective finite and valid\n- Re-evaluate final candidate to ensure no floating errors\nThese steps ensure compliance with all problem constraints.\n\nNow implementing the solution...",
